<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7. </span>Sensitivity analysis of hyper-parameters. Results are reported with the MRR metric.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5. </span><span id="S5.SS5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RQ4:</span> Efficiency and Effectiveness Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare retrieval latency and document-level recall (R@5<math id="S5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsf{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">D</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsf{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>) across representative baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.4. RQ3: Cross-domain Generalizability ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates a clear efficiency-effectiveness trade-off across retrieval paradigms. Single-step dense retrievers (<span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ColBERTv2</span>) achieve low latency but suffer from limited recall, while multi-step methods such as <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HippoRAG</span>+<span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IRCoT</span> improve effectiveness at the cost of substantial latency. In contrast, <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm</span>-Retriever consistently lies in the upper-left region across all datasets, achieving the highest or near-highest R@5<math id="S5.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsf{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">D</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsf{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> with sub-second retrieval time. Compared to graph-based baselines like <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SubgraphRAG</span> and <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GFM-RAG</span>, it delivers stronger retrieval quality without iterative graph traversal, benefiting from the IB-optimized subgraph selector that extracts a compact query-conditioned subgraph in a single forward pass.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.6. </span><span id="S5.SS6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RQ5:</span> Subgraph Visualization and Case Study</h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.4. RQ3: Cross-domain Generalizability ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> illustrates a representative multi-hop QA example from <span id="S5.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HotpotQA</span>.
<span id="S5.SS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm</span>-Retriever retrieves a compact subgraph that forms a clear reasoning chain from “Corliss Archer” to “Kiss and Tell”, “Shirley Temple”, and the correct government role “Chief of Protocol”.
Importantly, entities that are semantically related but irrelevant to the target reasoning path are excluded.
This case illustrates that <span id="S5.SS6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm</span>-Retriever identifies a minimal, query-aligned subgraph whose explicit relational paths directly support multi-hop inference, rather than aggregating loosely related evidence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.7. </span><span id="S5.SS7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RQ6:</span> Ablation and Sensitivity Analysis</h3>

<section id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Ablation.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We ablate <span id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">five</span> core modules of <span id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm</span>-Retriever: prototype alignment (<span id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proto</span>, Eq. (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.1. Generalized GFM as Cross-domain Retriever ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>)), information gain regularizer (<span id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">IGC</span>, Eq. (<a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4.1. Generalized GFM as Cross-domain Retriever ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), fine-tuning (<span id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tune</span>, Eq. (<a href="#S4.E18" title="In 4.2. Label-free IB Optimized Subgraph Selector ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>), the subgraph selector (<span id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SubG</span>, Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Label-free IB Optimized Subgraph Selector ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>), and the in-context relational prompting strategy (<span id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prompt</span>, Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Relation Paths Induced In-context Prompter ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>). As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.4. RQ3: Cross-domain Generalizability ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, removing any component consistently degrades performance on both retrieval and QA.
Specifically, removing <span id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proto+IGC</span> leads to clear drops in R@5<math id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsf{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">D</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsf{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>, degrading into the retriever in <span id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GFM-RAG</span>, sacrificing cross-domain alignment. Disabling <span id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tune</span> causes degradation, especially on QA, highlighting the importance of adapting to query-conditioned subgraph selection. Removing <span id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SubG</span> also results in substantial declines, corresponding to replacing the learned subgraph with a general retrieval strategy. Removing <span id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prompt</span> further reduces QA performance, whereas retrieval remains less affected, confirming that explicitly organizing relational paths into in-context prompts is important for translating structural evidence into effective multi-hop reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sensitivity.</span>
We study <span id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">five</span> weighting hyper-parameters: <math id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.1. Generalized GFM as Cross-domain Retriever ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), and <math id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E17" title="In Proposition 4.4 (Upper Bound of 𝐼(𝒢,𝒢_q)). ‣ 4.2. Label-free IB Optimized Subgraph Selector ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>).
Figure <a href="#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5.4. RQ3: Cross-domain Generalizability ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> reports the results measured by MRR. Overall, performance is sensitive to different values of the hyper-parameters and contains a reasonable range.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we rethink graph-based retrieval from a structural perspective and propose <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm</span>-Retriever, which directly learns query-specific subgraphs as reasoning evidence. By repurposing a pre-trained GFM as a cross-domain retriever and introducing a label-free information bottleneck for optimizing minimal yet sufficient subgraphs, our framework enables efficient and interpretable multi-hop reasoning. Extensive experiments demonstrate strong retrieval quality, QA performance, generalization, and efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
