<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.19.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><math id="A4.T1a.m190" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CIRCLE" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>●</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CIRCLE</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.19.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><math id="A4.T1a.m191" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ocircle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">○</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ocircle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.19.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><math id="A4.T1a.m192" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ocircle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">○</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ocircle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.19.12" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><math id="A4.T1a.m193" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ocircle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">○</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ocircle</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T1a.4.1.20" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.20.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><span id="A4.T1a.4.1.20.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm<span id="A4.T1a.4.1.20.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-Retriever</span></span></td>
<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.20.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><math id="A4.T1a.m194" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CIRCLE" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>●</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CIRCLE</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.20.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><math id="A4.T1a.m195" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CIRCLE" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>●</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CIRCLE</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.20.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><math id="A4.T1a.m196" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CIRCLE" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>●</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CIRCLE</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.20.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><math id="A4.T1a.m197" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CIRCLE" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>●</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CIRCLE</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.20.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><math id="A4.T1a.m198" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CIRCLE" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>●</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CIRCLE</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.20.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><math id="A4.T1a.m199" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CIRCLE" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>●</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CIRCLE</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.20.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><math id="A4.T1a.m200" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CIRCLE" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>●</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CIRCLE</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.20.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><math id="A4.T1a.m201" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CIRCLE" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>●</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CIRCLE</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.20.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><math id="A4.T1a.m202" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CIRCLE" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>●</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CIRCLE</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.20.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><math id="A4.T1a.m203" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CIRCLE" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>●</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CIRCLE</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T1a.4.1.20.12" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 2.5pt;"><math id="A4.T1a.m204" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CIRCLE" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>●</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CIRCLE</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare with <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">18</span> state-of-the-art baselines (and their combinations), categorized by <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">four</span> groups:
<span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Base LLM:</span> <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o-mini</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hurst et al., 2024</a>)</cite>;
<span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Single-step RAGs:</span> including <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BM25</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robertson and Walker, 1994</a>)</cite>, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Contriever</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Izacard et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GTR</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ColBERTv2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Santhanam et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RAPTOR</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sarthi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Proposition</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>;
<span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Graph-enhanced RAGs:</span> <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GraphRAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edge et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">G-Retriever</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LightRAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HippoRAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gutiérrez et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HippoRAG</span> <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gutiérrez et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SubgraphRAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PropRAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Han, 2025</a>)</cite>, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GFM-RAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>;
<span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.22" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(4) Multi-step RAGs:</span> including <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.23" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IRCoT</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Trivedi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.24" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FLARE</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.25" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Adaptive-RAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
A brief introduction is below.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Base LLM:</span>
uses a pre-trained large language model without fine-tuning or retrieval augmentation. It serves as the common generator for all retrieval-based methods, ensuring a fair comparison. We adopt <span id="A4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o-mini</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hurst et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which answers queries solely based on parametric knowledge.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Single-step RAGs:</span>
retrieve in a single pass, and are widely used due to their efficiency and general applicability. This category includes both sparse and dense retrievers:</p>
<ul id="A4.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BM25</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robertson and Walker, 1994</a>)</cite>: a classical probabilistic sparse retriever based on term frequency, document length normalization, and inverse document frequency, serving as a strong zero-shot baseline.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Contriever</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Izacard et al., 2022</a>)</cite>: an unsupervised dense retriever trained via contrastive learning, exhibiting strong zero-shot and cross-domain generalization without labeled data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GTR</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2022</a>)</cite>: a T5-based dual encoder that improves cross-domain retrieval by scaling model capacity with dot-product scoring, achieving high data efficiency.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ColBERTv2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Santhanam et al., 2022</a>)</cite>: a late-interaction retriever that performs token-level matching with compressed representations, balancing retrieval quality and memory efficiency.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RAPTOR</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sarthi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: a hierarchical retrieval method that organizes documents into recursive summaries, enabling retrieval across multiple abstraction levels for complex queries.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Proposition</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: a dense retriever that indexes text using fine-grained propositions as atomic units, improving retrieval precision and downstream QA under limited budgets.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Graph-enhanced RAGs:</span>
incorporate structured knowledge in the form of graphs to enhance retrieval and reasoning by explicitly modeling entities and relations, enabling multi-hop dependency modeling beyond flat text retrieval.</p>
<ul id="A4.I3" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GraphRAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edge et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: constructs an entity graph from documents and generates community-level summaries with LLMs, enabling corpus-level question answering beyond standard retrieval.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">G-Retriever</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: formulates retrieval as a soft Prize-Collecting Steiner Tree problem to identify query-relevant subgraphs in large graphs, improving reasoning while reducing hallucination.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LightRAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: integrates graph-aware indexing with flat text retrieval via a dual-level scheme, capturing both fine- and coarse-grained semantic dependencies efficiently.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HippoRAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gutiérrez et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: combines knowledge graphs with Personalized PageRank to support efficient one-step retrieval inspired by hippocampal memory, with strong multi-hop QA performance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I3.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HippoRAG</span> <span id="A4.I3.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gutiérrez et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: extends <span id="A4.I3.i5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HippoRAG</span> with deeper passage integration and improved LLM interaction, enhancing factual, associative, and sense-making memory.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I3.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I3.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I3.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I3.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SubgraphRAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>: retrieves compact KG subgraphs using triple scoring and structural distance encoding, but relies on fixed heuristics that limit query-adaptive reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I3.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I3.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I3.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I3.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PropRAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Han, 2025</a>)</cite>: replaces triples with context-rich propositions and performs beam search over proposition paths, improving zero-shot multi-hop retrieval.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I3.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I3.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I3.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I3.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GFM-RAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: employs a graph foundation model as a retriever over large heterogeneous graphs, but lacks explicit cross-domain semantic alignment and does not fully exploit KG reasoning structure at inference.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(4) Multi-step RAGs:</span>
iteratively interleave retrieval and reasoning to support complex multi-hop questions, gradually constructing reasoning chains. These methods are largely retriever-agnostic and can be combined with different RAG architectures.</p>
<ul id="A4.I4" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A4.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IRCoT</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Trivedi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>: interleaves retrieval with Chain-of-Thought reasoning, where each reasoning step guides the next retrieval. This design reduces hallucination and improves multi-hop QA. Notably, <span id="A4.I4.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IRCoT</span> is retriever-agnostic and can be paired with existing RAG methods to enable multi-step reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FLARE</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>: performs forward-looking active retrieval during generation by anticipating low-confidence content and retrieving evidence to refine it, improving factual consistency in long-form generation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Adaptive-RAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: dynamically selects between single-step and multi-step retrieval strategies based on predicted query complexity, balancing efficiency and reasoning capability across diverse queries.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3. </span>Experimental Setting Details</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce detailed experimental settings<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            Since standard benchmark datasets are adopted, and the same base LLM settings are used, the baseline results reported in this paper are taken directly from the original publications unless otherwise stated.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detailed Settings for Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. RQ1: Entity Retrieval Performance ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> (<span id="A4.SS3.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RQ1</span>).</span>
We evaluate entity- and document-level retrieval under a unified protocol. Baselines that output ranked lists are evaluated using Recall@2/5 on entities and documents (R@2/5<math id="A4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsf{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">E</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsf{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>, R@2/5<math id="A4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsf{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">D</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsf{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>). In contrast, <span id="A4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm</span>-Retriever returns a query-conditioned subgraph <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\mathbf{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>𝐪</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\mathbf{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> and uses its node set <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\mathbf{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>𝐪</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\mathbf{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> as retrieved entities, where the retrieval size is determined by the learned selector rather than an explicit top-<math id="A4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> cutoff (we set <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=5</annotation></semantics></math> for comparison).
Document retrieval is performed using the same entity-to-chunk inverted index across all methods. For multi-step baselines, we aggregate unique entities and documents across steps and evaluate Recall on the final ranked outputs. This setting decouples retrieval quality from downstream generation while maintaining consistent entity-document linking.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detailed Settings for Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. RQ2: Question Answering Performance ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> (<span id="A4.SS3.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RQ2</span>).</span>
We evaluate end-to-end QA by feeding the retrieved evidence from each method into the same base LLM, <span id="A4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o-mini</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hurst et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Single-step RAGs use top retrieved document chunks under the same budget as <span id="A4.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">RQ1</span>, while graph-enhanced methods serialize retrieved entities or subgraphs following their original designs. Multi-step methods such as <span id="A4.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IRCoT</span> interleave retrieval and reasoning, and concatenate evidence across steps. For <span id="A4.SS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm</span>-Retriever, the input is constructed from the query-specific minimal sufficient subgraph, including selected entities, relational paths, and linked document chunks. All methods share identical decoding settings and are evaluated without QA fine-tuning, using EM, F1, Precision, and Recall on test splits.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detailed Settings for Section <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4. RQ3: Cross-domain Generalizability ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> (<span id="A4.SS3.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RQ3</span>).</span>
The retrieval performance is evaluated on all seven cross-domain QA datasets, while generative QA is reported on three datasets that provide free-form answer annotations. For retrieval, we use document-level Recall@5 (R@5<math id="A4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsf{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">D</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsf{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>), reflecting a practical retrieval budget and better capturing evidence coverage under domain shift. For generative QA, we adopt ROUGE-L instead of EM or F1, as cross-domain answers are often longer and paraphrased, where sequence-level overlap more reliably reflects semantic faithfulness.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detailed Settings for Section <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5. RQ4: Efficiency and Effectiveness Analysis ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> (<span id="A4.SS3.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RQ4</span>).</span>
We evaluate retriever-side efficiency by measuring end-to-end retrieval latency together with document-level Recall@5 (R@5<math id="A4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsf{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">D</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsf{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>). Latency is measured from query input to document scoring completion, excluding LLM generation. For multi-step methods (<span id="A4.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, <span id="A4.SS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IRCoT</span>-based), latency includes all iterative retrieval steps, while for graph-based methods, offline indexing is excluded and online traversal is counted. All results are averaged over 1,000 runs to ensure fair comparison across different retrieval paradigms.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detailed Settings for Section <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6. RQ5: Subgraph Visualization and Case Study ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a> (<span id="A4.SS3.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RQ5</span>).</span>
We conduct a case study on a multi-hop question from <span id="A4.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HotpotQA</span>. The question asks for the government position held by the actress who portrayed “Corliss Archer” in the film “Kiss and Tell”. The supporting evidence spans multiple documents, including the film “Kiss and Tell (1945)”, the actress “Shirley Temple”, and biographical descriptions of her later public service role. We apply <span id="A4.SS3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm</span>-Retriever to this instance and visualize the query-conditioned subgraph produced by the subgraph selector. The visualization highlights the selected entities, relations, and explicit reasoning paths, and contrasts them with semantically related but query-irrelevant entities present in the original context. This case enables qualitative inspection of whether the retrieved subgraph is both minimal and sufficient for supporting multi-hop reasoning, with full metadata provided in Figure <a href="#A4.F1" title="Figure D.1 ‣ D.3. Experimental Setting Details ‣ Appendix D Experiment Details ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detailed Settings for Section <a href="#S5.SS7" title="5.7. RQ6: Ablation and Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a> (<span id="A4.SS3.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RQ6</span>).</span>
For ablation studies, to analyze the contribution of the core components in <span id="A4.SS3.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm</span>-Retriever, we construct <span id="A4.SS3.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">five</span> ablated variants (and their combinations) by removing the specific modules while keeping other settings unchanged. The variants are:</p>
<ul id="A4.I5" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A4.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm<span id="A4.I5.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-Retriever (<span id="A4.I5.i1.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o Proto</span>):</span></span> Removes the prototype-based semantic alignment loss <math id="A4.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{proto}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>proto</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{proto}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.1. Generalized GFM as Cross-domain Retriever ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), disabling cross-domain prototype supervision during retriever training.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm<span id="A4.I5.i2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-Retriever (<span id="A4.I5.i2.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o IGC</span>):</span></span> Removes the information gain contrast loss <math id="A4.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{IGC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>IGC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{IGC}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4.1. Generalized GFM as Cross-domain Retriever ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), preventing the retriever from being regularized toward informative semantic prototypes.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm<span id="A4.I5.i3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-Retriever (<span id="A4.I5.i3.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o Tune</span>):</span></span> Disables the fine-tuning stage defined in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E18" title="In 4.2. Label-free IB Optimized Subgraph Selector ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>), where the retriever is adapted to query-specific subgraph selection.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I5.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I5.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I5.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I5.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm<span id="A4.I5.i4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-Retriever (<span id="A4.I5.i4.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o SubG</span>):</span></span> Removes the entire subgraph selector module in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Label-free IB Optimized Subgraph Selector ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, reducing the model to entity-level retrieval without learning a minimal sufficient subgraph.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I5.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I5.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I5.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I5.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm<span id="A4.I5.i5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-Retriever (<span id="A4.I5.i5.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o Prompt</span>):</span></span> Removes the in-context relational prompting strategy described in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Relation Paths Induced In-context Prompter ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>. Retrieved entities and documents are still used, but relational paths are not explicitly reorganized into structured prompts for the LLM.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">All variants are evaluated under the same settings.
For hyper-parameter sensitivity analysis, we conduct sensitivity analysis on <span id="A4.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">five</span> key weighting hyper-parameters:</p>
<ul id="A4.I6" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A4.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\alpha_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\alpha_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.I6.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">:</span> controls weight of the binary cross-entropy retrieval <math id="A4.I6.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{BCE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>BCE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{BCE}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.1. Generalized GFM as Cross-domain Retriever ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), which helps relevance prediction for query-entity pairs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\alpha_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\alpha_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">:</span> controls the contribution of the ranking-based retrieval loss <math id="A4.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{rank}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>rank</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{rank}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.1. Generalized GFM as Cross-domain Retriever ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), enforcing a margin between relevant and irrelevant entities to improve retrieval robustness.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.I6.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\alpha_{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\alpha_{3}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.I6.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">:</span> weights the prototype-based semantic alignment loss <math id="A4.I6.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{proto}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>proto</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{proto}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.1. Generalized GFM as Cross-domain Retriever ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), regulating the strength of cross-domain semantic consistency during training.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I6.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I6.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I6.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.I6.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\beta_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\beta_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.I6.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">:</span> controls the minimality regularization term that penalizes the size of the selected subgraph in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E18" title="In 4.2. Label-free IB Optimized Subgraph Selector ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I6.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I6.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I6.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.I6.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\beta_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\beta_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.I6.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">:</span> controls the connectivity regularization term that encourages structural coherence of the selected subgraph in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E18" title="In 4.2. Label-free IB Optimized Subgraph Selector ‣ 4. Proposed Framework: Gfm-Retriever ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A4.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Each hyper-parameter is varied independently, and performance is reported using MRR on the validation set.</p>
</div>
