</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i18" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i18.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i18.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Adaptive-RAG</span>: <a href="https://github.com/starsuzi/Adaptive-RAG" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/starsuzi/Adaptive-RAG</a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For all baselines, if the same standard benchmark datasets and the same base LLM settings are used, the results reported in this paper are taken directly from the original
publications. When such settings are unavailable, we adopt the hyper-parameter configurations suggested in the original publications or provided by official implementations to ensure reasonable baseline performance.
To maintain a fair and controlled comparison, we keep other factors such as hidden size, number of layers, and architectural settings consistent with those used in our proposed framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3. </span>Hardware and Software Configurations</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct the experiments in a Linux-based server environment with the following hardware and software configurations.</p>
<ul id="A5.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Operating System:</span> Ubuntu 20.04 LTS.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CPU:</span> 10 Intel(R) Xeon(R) Platinum 8358 CPU @ 2.60GHz with 1TB DDR4 of Memory.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPU:</span> 8 NVIDIA Tesla A100 SMX4 with 80GB of Memory.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Software:</span> CUDA 12.4, Python 3.8.12, PyTorch<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                    <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                    
                    
                    
                  <a href="https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch</a>.</span></span></span> 1.9.1, PyTorch Geometric<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                    <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                    
                    
                    
                  <a href="https://github.com/pyg-team/pytorch_geometric" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/pyg-team/pytorch_geometric</a>.</span></span></span> 2.0.1.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Additional Results</h2>

<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1. </span>Impact of Domain-Specific Fine-Tuning</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further investigate and compare the cross-domain adaptability of retrievers, we fine-tune the most relevant baseline <span id="A6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GFM-RAG</span> and our <span id="A6.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm</span>-Retriever on the training split of its corresponding target dataset and evaluate on the same <span id="A6.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">seven</span> cross-domain benchmarks used in Section <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4. RQ3: Cross-domain Generalizability ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> (<span id="A6.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">RQ3</span>). Table  <a href="#A6.T1" title="Table F.1 ‣ F.1. Impact of Domain-Specific Fine-Tuning ‣ Appendix F Additional Results ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a> reports document retrieval performance using R@5<math id="A6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsf{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">D</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsf{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and Table <a href="#A6.T2" title="Table F.2 ‣ F.1. Impact of Domain-Specific Fine-Tuning ‣ Appendix F Additional Results ‣ Retrieving Minimal and Sufficient Reasoning Subgraphswith Graph Foundation Models for Path-aware GraphRAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.2</span></a> reports generative QA performance using ROUGE-L on the three datasets that support free-form answers. Results marked with “*” denote performance after domain-specific fine-tuning, while unmarked results correspond to the zero-shot setting in consistency with that in <span id="A6.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">RQ3</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Results show that domain-specific fine-tuning yields clear improvements for both <span id="A6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GFM-RAG</span> and <span id="A6.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm</span>-Retriever across retrieval and generative QA, indicating that both benefit from in-domain supervision. However, <span id="A6.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm</span>-Retriever consistently remains stronger than <span id="A6.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GFM-RAG</span> in both settings. Specifically, for cross-domain document retrieval, <span id="A6.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm</span>-Retriever outperforms <span id="A6.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GFM-RAG</span> before fine-tuning and retains this advantage after fine-tuning. The same trend holds for the generative QA, where <span id="A6.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm</span>-Retriever achieves higher ROUGE-L both zero-shot and fine-tuned. Overall, the results suggest that <span id="A6.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gfm</span>-Retriever not only generalizes better for cross-domain retrieval, but also provides a consistently stronger adaptation starting point under domain-specific fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table F.1. </span>Impact of domain-specific fine-tuning on cross-domain document retrieval (R@5<math id="A6.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsf{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">D</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsf{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> %). Results marked with “*” denote the fine-tuned performance.</figcaption>
